From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm: the disk image takes a conversation state at any position

cuda_disk_image.parts refused every state whose position was not a multiple of the 4-row pool (`SnapshotShape`), and the
store counts such a save as skipped, so a conversation state kept at a request start or at a prompt's end reached the
disk only when it happened to land on a multiple of 4: three in four did not. With --learn-conversations and
TF_GLM_REQUEST_STARTS=1 on three GB10s at TP=3, a restart left a 596k-token conversation resuming from the 595,968-row
state its first turn kept instead of the 619,434-row endpoint its second turn kept (the endpoint's save was skipped), and
32k and 120k-token conversations whose only kept states were off a pool's edge (32,035 and 120,601 rows) resumed from
nothing and prefilled cold. Six saves were skipped in the first boot of that run, four in the second and five in the third.

The image has no other reason to refuse such a state. The store's `rows.image.valid` takes a pooled part of between
`at / per` and `at / per + 1` rows, and `parts` already gives the whole pools as rows by position and leaves the pool
past them, with the spare row, as the state's own bytes (`cache`). The pooled-key kernel writes only a completed pool
(`tf_glm_pool_keys` returns when `4 * p + 3 > pos + rows - 1`), so a prompt pass that ends inside a pool leaves that
pool's row unwritten, which is what `unwritten` already assumes of the rows past the whole pools; a pass that starts
inside a pool reads that pool's earlier rows from the index rings, and the image carries the rings. So `parts` takes
every position from 1 to the capacity, and `longest` is the capacity.

An image written at a position on a pool's edge is unchanged, and so is the words' format: only states the old `parts`
refused are new files. Replies are the cold pass's: a kept state resumes the same tokens whether it came from RAM or
from the image, and the disk tier's own check compares every resumed reply with a cold server's.

Receipts: host tests of the image at positions on and inside a pool (the lease bytes against the snapshot plan, the rows
by position and the own parts, a round trip through the lease into another sequence, and the probe hash's unwritten
spans). The GPU receipts (the disk tier's resume check with request starts on) are not in this header.

New here, not a re-seat: written at v1.2.9 on top of this repo's stack (patches/engine, pr1411, cache-salt, learn-starts
and solo-cuts).

---

